window.AADS_TASK_RESOURCES={"states":["Alabama","Alaska","Arizona","Arkansas","California","Colorado","Connecticut","Delaware","Florida","Georgia","Hawaii","Idaho","Illinois","Indiana","Iowa","Kansas","Kentucky","Louisiana","Maine","Maryland","Massachusetts","Michigan","Minnesota","Mississippi","Missouri","Montana","Nebraska","Nevada","New Hampshire","New Jersey","New Mexico","New York","North Carolina","North Dakota","Ohio","Oklahoma","Oregon","Pennsylvania","Rhode Island","South Carolina","South Dakota","Tennessee","Texas","Utah","Vermont","Virginia","Washington","West Virginia","Wisconsin","Wyoming"],"records":[{"title":"What to Provide Before Requesting an Apostille or Authentication","url":"./guide-apostille-request-checklist.html","topic":"international","states":[],"question":"Home/What to Provide Before Requesting an Apostille or AuthenticationREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereIdentify the document’s origin, destination country and receiving party’s instructions before choosing a processing route. The word “apostille” alone does not define the whole request.Start with origin and destinationUSAGov explains that apostilles and authentication certificates verify signatures, stamps or seals on documents, and that the destination affects the required route. Its guidance distinguishes state-issued records from federal records and directs users to the relevant authorities. The U.S. Department of State also publishes separate preparation and submission guidance for authentication and apostille requests.These distinctions make a complete intake important. A document issued by a state authority and one issued by a federal authority should not be routed on the assumption that the same office handles both.The information to collectDocument type: identify the actual record, such as a corporate document, vital record, court record or other specified document.Issuing authority: state who issued or certified it and the state or federal origin. Supply a clear copy for route review when appropriate.Country of use: name the destination and provide the receiving party’s written instructions.Available version: identify whether you hold an original, certified copy, photocopy or electronic record.Additional requirements: include any translation, issuance-date, original-format or consular requirement stated by the recipient.Timing and delivery: give the date needed, delivery destination and any deadline dependency.Do not change the document before reviewAvoid adding a notarization, detaching a certification or mailing an irreplaceable original solely because a generic checklist suggests it. Preparation requirements depend on the document and authority. Confirm what is needed for this specific request before altering or submitting the record.Illustrative request wording“Please review the attached document copy and receiving-party instructions for use in the country identified below. Confirm the required document version, processing authorities, anticipated timing, fees and return-delivery arrangements before we send the original.”What should the confirmation cover?Keep a written record of the agreed route, what the client supplies, any documents to be retrieved, and what is being returned. If recipient requirements remain uncertain, identify the open question rather than promising acceptance. AADS can coordinate the confirmed processing work; the issuing and receiving authorities retain their respective roles.What does an apostille verify?It relates to the signature, seal or official capacity associated with the public document. It should not be represented as validating every factual assertion in that document.Official sources and requirementsUSAGov — Authenticate a U.S. document for use abroad ↗U.S. Department of State — Document authentication ↗Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"USAGov — Authenticate a U.S. document for use abroad ↗","url":"https://www.usa.gov/authenticate-us-document"},{"label":"U.S. Department of State — Document authentication ↗","url":"https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/replace-certify-docs/authenticate-your-document.html"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["international"]},{"title":"Florida Public-Record Research: UCC, Property and Municipal Requests","url":"./guide-florida-public-records.html","topic":"general","states":["Florida"],"question":"Home/Florida Public-Record Research: UCC, Property and Municipal RequestsNationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub →Florida’s Department of State directs UCC forms, filings, searches, copies and certification requests to the Florida Secured Transaction Registry. Corporate entity information remains a separate Sunbiz service.Current official UCC guidanceSTATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start with the Source MapUse this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope.Which Source Answers Which Question?Research componentResponsible sourceDefine before orderingCentral UCC filings and searchesThe Florida Secured Transaction Registry, identified by the Department of StateExact debtor names, certification, copies and the search through dateRecorded property instrumentsThe responsible county Clerk or recording officeProperty, owners, search scope, deeds and specified encumbrance recordsMunicipal and building recordsResponsible city, county department or utility providerCode, permit, occupancy, utilities and assessments as separate componentsState/county and federal litigationNamed court clerks and separately defined federal sourcesCourts, names, period, categories, dockets and copiesKeep the Central and County Requests DistinctFlorida’s Department of State directs UCC forms, filings, searches, copies and certification requests to its designated registry. That central UCC request should not be used as a label for every property or county-record inquiry. Identify fixture or real-property record work separately when instructed.Identify the County and the Local RespondersFlagler County’s official-records description includes deeds, mortgages, assignments, liens and other instruments. Hillsborough County’s building-record guidance uses separate retrieval routes and date ranges for permits and occupancy records. These illustrate different sources; they do not define a standard package for every Florida property.Ask for the Actual DocumentsState whether you need a UCC search report, a certified search response, document images, a recorded instrument or a departmental response. For a certificate of occupancy or permit, provide the specific document type, property identifiers and known permit details. Older or unavailable records may need direct follow-up.Request ChecklistExact subject names and the requested central UCC search and copy requirements.Property address, parcel identifier, county, owner period and requested title scope.Municipal components and known city, county or utility providers.Named courts or federal districts and any docket or certified-copy requirements.Closing or matter deadline, delivery requirements and separately confirmed fees.Example Request“For [entity] and [Florida property], please quote the central UCC search with [certification/copies], the requested county property research covering [depth], and separate [code/permit/utility/assessment] inquiries. Identify the responsible offices, search through dates and outstanding responses. Our deadline is [date].”Make the Result AssessableAsk for a report identifying the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual request.Answers for Your RequestDoes the central Florida UCC registry replace county property research?No. The central registry handles its UCC records. Recorded property instruments and requested local-department responses are different sources and must be defined separately.Are all Florida permit or occupancy records available online?Availability depends on the authority, record type and date. Hillsborough County’s instructions, for example, identify different retrieval routes for different periods. Confirm the responsible authority and required document.Official sources and requirementsFlorida Department of State — UCC information ↗Florida Secured Transaction Registry — help and scope ↗Flagler County Clerk — official records ↗Hillsborough County — building permitting records ↗Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.Related Services and GuidesSERVICEUCC and Lien ResearchDefine the search and filing evidence needed.Explore →SERVICECivil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court ResearchSpecify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents.Explore →SERVICEReal Estate Title and Municipal Lien ResearchCoordinate property records and local responses.Explore →GLOSSARYPublic Records GlossaryUnderstand the terms used in your request.Explore →The Right Support for Your Next RequestGet the research, filings and supporting documents your request needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"Florida Department of State — UCC information ↗","url":"https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/ucc-information"},{"label":"Florida Secured Transaction Registry — help and scope ↗","url":"https://floridaucc.com/help"},{"label":"Flagler County Clerk — official records ↗","url":"https://flaglerclerk.gov/records/official-records/"},{"label":"Hillsborough County — building permitting records ↗","url":"https://hcfl.gov/businesses/development-services-records/building-permitting-records-searches"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["ucc-liens","corporate","courts","property"]},{"title":"Georgia Public-Record Research: UCC, Recorded Liens and Court Sources","url":"./guide-georgia-public-records.html","topic":"general","states":["Georgia"],"question":"Home/Georgia Public-Record Research: UCC, Recorded Liens and Court SourcesNationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub →Georgia’s GSCCCA UCC index offers name, filing-number, county/date and secured-party searches. Its notice effective 1 November 2026 requires UCC-11 information requests to be submitted electronically through Certified Search Request.Current official UCC guidanceGSCCCA UCC-11 electronic-submission noticeSTATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start with the Source MapUse this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope.Which Source Answers Which Question?Research componentResponsible sourceDefine before orderingUCC record researchGSCCCA UCC index and certified-search routesExact debtor names, search method, certification and copiesReal estate, recorded liens and platsGSCCCA indexes and the responsible county custodianCounty, property and instrument details, index coverage and copiesState-system civil case researchThe relevant Georgia court and clerkCourt class, county, names, period and case categoriesFederal litigation and bankruptcySeparately identified federal court or PACER scopeDistricts or index scope, subjects, dockets and documentsTreat Each Index as a Defined SourceGSCCCA lists separate UCC, real-estate, lien and plat search resources, including a certified UCC search request route. Select the index and requested results and documents explicitly. An index result and a certified response or underlying instrument are different records.Name the Georgia Court TypeGeorgia’s judicial guidance describes several trial-court classes. Superior, state and magistrate court sources can involve different records. The request should identify the court classes and counties chosen by the reviewing team; the phrase “county litigation search” alone leaves those choices open.Confirm Coverage and Obtain the RecordThe judicial directory helps identify the relevant court contact. County custody, available indexes, historical coverage, images and certification requirements need confirmation for the actual request. Federal district and bankruptcy records are a separate research path.Request ChecklistEach subject name and the requested GSCCCA index, certification and copy requirements.County, property identifiers, instrument references and title scope where relevant.The actual superior, state, magistrate or other court sources requested.Civil case categories, period, pending/closed instructions and any known case numbers.Separate federal/bankruptcy scope, desired documents, client reference and deadline.Example Request“For [subject] in [Georgia county], please quote the specified GSCCCA UCC/lien research and civil litigation in [named court classes]. Include [period/status categories], located case references and [documents/copies]. Separately confirm any federal or bankruptcy research. Identify the indexes and courts actually searched.”Make the Result AssessableAsk for a report identifying the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual request.Answers for Your RequestDoes a GSCCCA recorded-lien search replace civil litigation research?No. Specify the recorded indexes and the court case sources separately. They answer different record questions and may have different coverage and results and documents.Which Georgia courts should be included?Your reviewing team should identify the appropriate court classes and counties for the request. AADS confirms the requested sources and results and documents; one court search should not be represented as every Georgia court.Official sources and requirementsGSCCCA — UCC, real estate, lien and plat indexes ↗Georgia Courts — trial-court types ↗Georgia Courts — official directory and records resources ↗PACER — finding federal court cases ↗Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.Related Services and GuidesSERVICEUCC and Lien ResearchDefine the search and filing evidence needed.Explore →SERVICECivil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court ResearchSpecify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents.Explore →SERVICEReal Estate Title and Municipal Lien ResearchCoordinate property records and local responses.Explore →GLOSSARYPublic Records GlossaryUnderstand the terms used in your request.Explore →The Right Support for Your Next RequestGet the research, filings and supporting documents your request needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"GSCCCA — UCC, real estate, lien and plat indexes ↗","url":"https://www.gsccca.org/search"},{"label":"Georgia Courts — trial-court types ↗","url":"https://georgiacourts.gov/a2j/self-help-resources/navigating-the-courts/"},{"label":"Georgia Courts — official directory and records resources ↗","url":"https://georgiacourts.gov/data-and-records/"},{"label":"PACER — finding federal court cases ↗","url":"https://www.pacer.uscourts.gov/find-case"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["ucc-liens","corporate","courts","property"]},{"title":"Certificate of Good Standing, Online Status & Certified Copies: What to Order","url":"./guide-good-standing-certified-copy.html","topic":"corporate","states":["Delaware"],"question":"Home/Certificate of Good Standing, Online Status & Certified Copies: What to OrderREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereMatch the document to the receiving party’s requirement. A certificate of good standing, an online status result and a certified copy serve different needs.Ask for the requirement before ordering“Please send the company documents” leaves important choices open. Obtain the wording from the lender, law firm, authority or other recipient. Confirm the entity, issuing jurisdiction, document type, certification and date requirements before requesting production.Delaware provides a useful example: its online-status guidance distinguishes an online status result from an official certificate of good standing. It also explains that a status-and-history result does not supply images of the underlying filed documents. Other states may use different names and processes, so use the relevant issuing office’s instructions.Separate the results and documentsRequested itemWhat to clarifyOnline entity or status resultDoes the recipient accept a database result, or require an official certificate of good standing?Certificate of good standing (or the issuing office’s equivalent)Which issuing state, certificate of good standing form and issuance-date requirement apply?Copy of a filed documentWhich formation document, amendment, filing date or document number is needed?Certified copyWhich underlying document must be certified by the office holding the record?Prepare a complete corporate document requestConfirm the entity. Provide the exact legal name and file number if available. A similar company name is not enough to identify the intended record confidently.Name the jurisdiction. Specify the issuing state and distinguish a formation-state requirement from a requirement involving another registration.Specify the item. Identify whether you need a certificate of good standing, a plain copy or a certified copy, and any requested version or historical filing.Keep the recipient’s conditions. Include any document-age, original-format or delivery condition rather than assuming every certificate of good standing is accepted.Identify international use. If the document is going abroad, provide the destination country and recipient instructions so any separate authentication route can be reviewed.Illustrative request wording“The receiving party requires an official certificate of good standing for the entity identified below, issued within its stated date window. Please confirm the available form, fees, anticipated timing and delivery format before ordering.”AADS can coordinate the specified records and filings. The issuing authority determines what it can issue, and the receiving party determines whether the supplied item satisfies its requirement.Official sources and requirementsDelaware Division of Corporations — Online status and official certificates ↗Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"Delaware Division of Corporations — Online status and official certificates ↗","url":"https://corp.delaware.gov/onlinestatus/"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["corporate"]},{"title":"Title Search and Municipal Research: What Should the Request Include?","url":"./guide-municipal-research-scope.html","topic":"property","states":[],"question":"Home/Title Search and Municipal Research: What Should the Request Include?REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereTreat recorded land records and local departmental responses as distinct parts of the research plan. Define the records your closing file needs before work begins.Why separate sources matterYour request identifies the property-specific departments, records and responses required for the request.For a closing checklist, the practical question is which components are included in the order and which office or provider supplies each response. A title abstract and a municipal response should not be treated as interchangeable documents.Write down the componentsResearch componentWhat to specifyRecorded property recordsParcel identity, title-search scope, relevant owner or instrument references, and copies required.Permits and codeThe requested status information, record period where relevant, and supporting case or permit records.Utilities and assessmentsThe requested account or property-balance research and the responsible providers, when known.Zoning documentsWhether the request needs an available online classification, an official verification letter or specified supporting documents.Give the researcher a usable property identityInclude the street address, county, state and parcel number. Supply a legal description, owner name or prior instrument reference when available. If the property contains several parcels, identify whether the request applies to each one. An address alone may leave the scope unclear.Plan for partial responsesAsk how separately outstanding responses will be identified. If an office has not answered, the status should remain pending; it should not be converted into a finding of no balance, violation or open permit. Confirm whether delivery is expected as a complete package or in clearly labeled stages.Illustrative intake checklistProperty and parcel identifiers, including any multiple-parcel scope.Requested recorded-record research and municipal lien requirements.Known government offices and separate utility or service providers.Supporting copies, official-letter requirements and date needed.Instructions for reporting unanswered requests or unavailable records.Does a municipal search replace a title search?Do not assume it does. Confirm the required sources and components with the ordering team. AADS reports the agreed factual research and responses; legal interpretation and title-insurance decisions remain with the appropriate professionals.Official sources and requirementsCity of Orlando — Property information and separate record sources ↗Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"City of Orlando — Property information and separate record sources ↗","url":"https://www.orlando.gov/Our-Government/Records-and-Documents/Property-Information"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["property"]},{"title":"North Carolina Public-Record Research: UCC, Deeds and Court Records","url":"./guide-north-carolina-public-records.html","topic":"general","states":["North Carolina"],"question":"Home/North Carolina Public-Record Research: UCC, Deeds and Court RecordsNationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub →North Carolina’s Secretary of State distinguishes free UCC research from an office search requested on UCC-11. Its UCC section maintains UCC and federal lien documents; deed and court requests require separate source instructions.Current official UCC guidanceSTATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start with the Source MapUse this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope.Which Source Answers Which Question?Research componentResponsible sourceDefine before orderingCentral UCC recordsNorth Carolina Secretary of State UCC sectionDebtor names, search/certification requirements, UCC-11 where requested and copiesDeeds and recorded instrumentsThe responsible county Register of DeedsCounty, property, owner names, instrument references and periodCourt-related land records and civil casesThe relevant Clerk of Superior Court and court sourcesCase categories, names, court, period and copiesFederal cases and bankruptcySeparately defined PACER or federal court scopeDistricts or index scope, subject names and requested recordsSpecify the UCC Results and DocumentsThe Secretary of State maintains a UCC section and publishes its forms, including a UCC-11 information request. An interactive lookup, requested official response and copies of specified filings should not be treated as interchangeable. Identify the required results and documents and search through date.Separate Deeds from Court RecordsNorth Carolina’s land-record guidance identifies both county Register of Deeds offices and county Clerks of Superior Court as sources for different records affecting land. A deed copy or a single index search does not answer every property or litigation question.Use Court Search Results as a Starting PointThe Judicial Branch explains access to case information and how to request court files and copies from the clerk. Identify the court, case number and particular documents needed. Online access and a docket listing are different from obtaining an official or certified record.Request ChecklistExact debtor or entity names, requested UCC search response and copy requirements.County, property address, parcel identifier, owners and defined search scope.Requested Register of Deeds records and any court-source components.Court, case categories, period and specified documents or certifications.Separate federal/bankruptcy instructions, client reference and date needed.Example Request“For [subject/property] in [North Carolina county], please quote [defined UCC response and copies], the requested Register of Deeds research, and civil court research in [named court/source] for [period]. Obtain [specified documents/certifications] and identify source dates and unavailable records.”Make the Result AssessableAsk for a report identifying the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual request.Answers for Your RequestIs a deed search the same as all North Carolina land-related research?No. The Secretary of State’s land-record guidance identifies different records at the county Register of Deeds and the Clerk of Superior Court. Specify the offices and record categories your reviewing team requires.What should I specify for a UCC-11 or other UCC request?Identify the exact debtor name, office, search response or certification, supporting copies and through-date requirement. Confirm the available request method and results and documents with the filing source.Official sources and requirementsNorth Carolina Secretary of State — UCC section ↗North Carolina Secretary of State — UCC forms including UCC-11 ↗North Carolina Secretary of State — land-record offices ↗North Carolina Judicial Branch — obtaining court records ↗PACER — finding federal court cases ↗Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.Related Services and GuidesSERVICEUCC and Lien ResearchDefine the search and filing evidence needed.Explore →SERVICECivil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court ResearchSpecify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents.Explore →SERVICEReal Estate Title and Municipal Lien ResearchCoordinate property records and local responses.Explore →GLOSSARYPublic Records GlossaryUnderstand the terms used in your request.Explore →The Right Support for Your Next RequestGet the research, filings and supporting documents your request needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"North Carolina Secretary of State — UCC section ↗","url":"https://www.sosnc.gov/divisions/uniform_commercial_code"},{"label":"North Carolina Secretary of State — UCC forms including UCC-11 ↗","url":"https://www.sosnc.gov/forms/by_title/_uniform_commercial_code"},{"label":"North Carolina Secretary of State — land-record offices ↗","url":"https://www.sosnc.gov/frequently_asked_questions/by_title/_land_records_deeds_who_owns_land"},{"label":"North Carolina Judicial Branch — obtaining court records ↗","url":"https://www.nccourts.gov/help-topics/court-records/obtaining-court-records"},{"label":"PACER — finding federal court cases ↗","url":"https://www.pacer.uscourts.gov/find-case"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["ucc-liens","corporate","courts","property"]},{"title":"OFAC Potential Matches: Preparing the Search and Reviewing the Record","url":"./guide-ofac-potential-matches.html","topic":"general","states":[],"question":"Home/OFAC Potential Matches: Preparing the Search and Reviewing the RecordREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start HereAn OFAC search can return similar names. Record the search scope and available identifiers so the client’s compliance team can assess potential matches.Define the Names Before the SearchList each individual and entity separately. Provide exact names, known aliases, relevant countries and identifying details already available for the transaction. Confirm which lists are in scope and what search documentation your reviewer requires.Understand a Potential MatchOFAC’s tool uses approximate name matching against its SDN and consolidated non-SDN lists. Similar names can return results for different parties. OFAC’s match-assessment guidance calls for comparison with available identifying details and escalation under the organization’s compliance procedures.Keep a Useful Search RecordSubject names and aliases submitted.Agreed lists, source, search date and available search settings.Result entries and relevant identifying details.Supporting search evidence, unresolved questions and the reviewing contact.A Useful Request“Please document an OFAC list search for [names and aliases], with [identifying details available]. Confirm the lists in scope and include the date, search evidence and potential matches. Send identity questions to [reviewing contact] before treating a result as resolved.”Keep Screening Separate from Transaction ApprovalName screening is one input to a broader sanctions assessment. Ownership and geographic restrictions can require additional review. A result with no matching name does not authorize a transaction; the client’s legal or compliance team determines the outcome.Official sources and requirementsOFAC — Sanctions List Search tool ↗OFAC FAQ 5 — Assessing potential name matches ↗Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"OFAC — Sanctions List Search tool ↗","url":"https://ofac.treasury.gov/sanctions-list-search-tool"},{"label":"OFAC FAQ 5 — Assessing potential name matches ↗","url":"https://ofac.treasury.gov/faqs/5"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["general"]},{"title":"Choosing a Property Research Report: Owner, Vesting, Mortgage or Defined-Depth Search","url":"./guide-property-report-options.html","topic":"property","states":[],"question":"Home/Choosing a Property Research Report: Owner, Vesting, Mortgage or Defined-Depth SearchREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start HereName the record questions your file needs answered, then confirm the owners, period, sources and supporting copies included in the request.A Report Name Is Only the Starting PointCurrent-owner, two-owner and full-search labels can conceal different scopes. Specify the property and parcel, relevant owner names, requested period and record categories. Agree the search scope with your legal or title team before ordering.Match the Request to the Results and DocumentsLegal and vesting deed report: recorded deed reference, owner names, vesting wording and legal description.Open-mortgage research: located mortgage instruments and requested related assignments, modifications or releases.Current-owner / O&E research: agreed ownership period, encumbrance categories and copies.Two-owner or defined-depth research: the specified owners, years or chain, with included record categories stated.Tax, municipal and zoning research: separately identified offices and components.Recorded Documents and Other SourcesFlagler County’s official-records guidance, for example, lists deeds, mortgages, assignments, satisfactions, plats, liens and other recorded instruments. That list describes a record source. It does not make each item part of every report or replace separate departmental and tax inquiries.A Useful Request“For [address/parcel] in [county/state], please quote [report type] covering [owners/period], including [record categories and copies]. Separately identify [tax/municipal lien requirements] and any unavailable records. Our client reference is [reference] and the requested date is [date].”Keep the Result and the Opinion DistinctThe report records factual findings within the agreed sources and scope. It does not insure title, establish loan ownership or decide the legal effect of an instrument. Refer questions about depth, gaps or enforceability to the reviewing professional.Official sources and requirementsFlagler County Clerk — Official Records ↗Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"Flagler County Clerk — Official Records ↗","url":"https://flaglerclerk.gov/records/official-records/"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["property"]},{"title":"How to Prepare a Multistate UCC and Lien Search Request","url":"./guide-ucc-search-scope.html","topic":"ucc-liens","states":["California"],"question":"Home/How to Prepare a Multistate UCC and Lien Search RequestREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereStart with the name, sources, jurisdictions and results and documents—not simply “a nationwide search.” A written scope gives the researcher and reviewing team the same reference point.Begin with the request checklist“UCC and liens” can describe several different requests. Before ordering, identify the record categories your reviewing team requires and which agencies or courts should be included. A state UCC search should not be assumed to include county records, litigation or bankruptcy research unless these are expressly part of the request.The California Secretary of State illustrates why naming instructions matter: its UCC information-request guidance calls for a separate request for each debtor name or variation and notes that other filing offices may offer different information options. This is a California example, not a rule to apply unchanged in every state.Build a request the next person can useIdentify the subject. Provide the exact name, entity type and known file identifiers. List former names or other variations separately; avoid leaving the researcher to infer which names are authorized.List the jurisdictions and sources. State which states, counties and courts are requested. If you need help identifying source options, ask for scope review before treating the request as final.Define the record categories. Specify UCC records, tax liens, judgments, litigation or bankruptcy as applicable to your team’s instructions.Define the output. Say whether an official UCC search certificate, plain copies, certified copies, a case index or particular documents are required. Identify copy limits.State the timing. Include the date needed and any search-period or effective-date requirements. Ask how source timing affects the results and documents.Illustrative request wording“Please review the attached subject-name and jurisdiction list for a UCC and lien search quote. The checklist identifies the requested sources, UCC search certificate and copy requirements. Please confirm scope, anticipated timing and any source limitations before proceeding.”This is an intake example, not a completed search or a recommendation of legal scope. Attach a clear checklist rather than placing several unstructured names and locations in an email thread.Check the delivery against the requestRetain the agreed scope with the returned work. Confirm that the report identifies the names searched, sources, relevant search dates and any outstanding items. A record not located in the specified research should not be restated as a universal finding that no obligation or lien exists.Does a UCC search prove priority?No legal conclusion about priority or enforceability follows simply from ordering a search. AADS supplies the factual records and requested research; the client’s legal team assesses their significance.Official sources and requirementsCalifornia Secretary of State — UCC information requests ↗Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"California Secretary of State — UCC information requests ↗","url":"https://www.sos.ca.gov/business-programs/ucc/information-request"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["ucc-liens"]},{"title":"UCC Filing Acknowledgment or Search to Reflect: What to Request","url":"./guide-ucc-search-to-reflect.html","topic":"ucc-liens","states":["Washington"],"question":"Home/UCC Filing Acknowledgment or Search to Reflect: What to RequestREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start HereKeep the filing acknowledgment and the later indexed search result distinct. Identify the filing, exact debtor name and search through date your review requires.Start with the Filing EvidenceProvide the office’s filing acknowledgment, financing statement number, exact debtor name and filing date. Identify whether the request concerns an initial statement or a later amendment. Ask for the particular record or indexed-search result your reviewer needs.Request the Search SeparatelyA search to reflect is a check for the identified filing in the filing office’s searchable records. Specify the office, exact search name, certification, copies and required through date. Indexing and the scope of the response depend on the office.A Washington ExampleWashington’s rules describe a search requested with an initial financing statement being conducted when relevant records through that filing date can be retrieved. The state’s search-response rules identify the search name, through date and returned filings. This is an example of one jurisdiction’s process, not a nationwide timing promise.A Useful Request“Please obtain a search to reflect financing statement [number] filed in [office] on [date] under [exact debtor name]. Include [certification/copies required], identify the through date and return the filing acknowledgment with the result.”Review the Record, Then Resolve QuestionsCompare the requested filing number and debtor information with the response. Flag an unavailable record or mismatch for follow-up rather than treating it as proof of legal invalidity. A filing acknowledgment and indexed search do not decide priority, perfection or enforceability.Official sources and requirementsWashington WAC 308-391-502 — Search timing ↗Washington WAC 308-391-505 — Search responses ↗Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"Washington WAC 308-391-502 — Search timing ↗","url":"https://app.leg.wa.gov/wac/default.aspx?cite=308-391-502"},{"label":"Washington WAC 308-391-505 — Search responses ↗","url":"https://app.leg.wa.gov/wac/default.aspx?cite=308-391-505"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["ucc-liens"]},{"title":"Washington Public-Record Research: UCC, Entity and County Sources","url":"./guide-washington-public-records.html","topic":"general","states":["Washington"],"question":"Home/Washington Public-Record Research: UCC, Entity and County SourcesNationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub →Washington UCC filing and UCC-11 searches are handled by the Department of Licensing, while business-entity records are handled by the Secretary of State. Its UCC forms distinguish information requests from amendments and information statements.Current official UCC guidanceSTATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start with the Source MapUse this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope.Which Source Answers Which Question?Research componentResponsible sourceDefine before orderingCentral UCC recordsWashington Department of LicensingDebtor names, filing references, search type and copiesBusiness entity recordsSecretary of State Corporations and Charities Filing System (CCFS)Entity name or UBI, filing history and the required certificate or copyRecorded property documentsThe responsible county recording officeProperty, instrument references, owner period and copiesCourt case information and documentsWashington court search resources and the court of recordCourt, names, period, case number and underlying documentsUse the Correct State OfficeWashington’s UCC filing and search guidance is published by the Department of Licensing. Corporate entity records are maintained through the Secretary of State’s CCFS. These are separate systems; an entity-status lookup is not a UCC search.Keep the Acknowledgment and Search Result TogetherProvide the filing acknowledgment and number, together with any separately requested search-to-reflect requirements, so your reviewing team receives the appropriate evidence.Check County and Court CoverageThurston County’s recording guidance is one example of a county property source and distinguishes indexed information from documents available online. Washington Courts also explains that its name/case search is reference information; the official case record and document copies are held by the court of record.Request ChecklistExact debtor names and the Department of Licensing UCC request type.Legal entity name, UBI if known and the exact CCFS document or status certificate needed.County, property identifiers and any known recording references.Court and case information, period, dockets and copies needed.Filing acknowledgment, search through-date requirement, client reference and deadline.Example Request“Please quote a Washington Department of Licensing UCC search for [exact name], with [certification/copies], and separately retrieve [entity document] from CCFS. For [county/property], include [specified records]. For court work, identify [court/period] and the documents required. Keep each source and through date distinct.”Make the Result AssessableAsk for a report identifying the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual request.Answers for Your RequestDoes Washington’s Secretary of State handle the central UCC search described here?Washington’s Department of Licensing provides the central UCC filing and search service. The Secretary of State’s CCFS is the separate source for corporate entity records.Does the Washington name-and-case search provide the full official case file?Washington Courts states that the search is reference information. The court of record maintains the official case record, and requested copies should be obtained through the appropriate court process.Official sources and requirementsWashington Department of Licensing — UCC filing and searches ↗Washington Secretary of State — CCFS records tools ↗Washington Courts — name and case search limits ↗Thurston County Auditor — property records ↗Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.Related Services and GuidesSERVICEUCC and Lien ResearchDefine the search and filing evidence needed.Explore →SERVICECivil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court ResearchSpecify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents.Explore →SERVICEReal Estate Title and Municipal Lien ResearchCoordinate property records and local responses.Explore →GLOSSARYPublic Records GlossaryUnderstand the terms used in your request.Explore →The Right Support for Your Next RequestGet the research, filings and supporting documents your request needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"Washington Department of Licensing — UCC filing and searches ↗","url":"https://dol.wa.gov/professional-licenses/uniform-commercial-code-ucc/ucc-online-filing-and-searches"},{"label":"Washington Secretary of State — CCFS records tools ↗","url":"https://www.sos.wa.gov/corporations-charities/frequently-asked-questions-faqs/online-filing-system-tools-resources"},{"label":"Washington Courts — name and case search limits ↗","url":"https://www.courts.wa.gov/index.cfm?fa=home.contentDisplay&location=nameAndCaseSearch"},{"label":"Thurston County Auditor — property records ↗","url":"https://www.thurstoncountywa.gov/departments/auditor/recording-services/look-property-records"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["ucc-liens","corporate","courts","property"]},{"title":"Zoning Letters, Certificates of Occupancy and Permit Copies: Preparing Your Request","url":"./guide-zoning-permit-records.html","topic":"general","states":[],"question":"Home/Zoning Letters, Certificates of Occupancy and Permit Copies: Preparing Your RequestREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026Start HereIdentify the property, responsible authority and exact document or question. Zoning responses, occupancy records and permit copies are separate requests.Identify the Correct AuthorityProvide the address, parcel number, city and county. Identify the department or jurisdiction already known to be responsible. A mailing address alone may not identify the office that holds zoning or building records.For a Zoning Verification LetterState the current or intended use and the specific questions the recipient wants answered. Supply the recipient’s wording or checklist when available. Do not assume a general zoning letter answers every question about a proposed use, variances or compliance.For Occupancy or Permit RecordsIdentify whether you need an existing certificate of occupancy, permit copy, inspection record or another named document. Provide permit numbers, construction dates and prior addresses when known. Ask the authority to identify unavailable documents rather than assuming every building has the same records.A Hillsborough County ExampleAvailable records and retrieval procedures are confirmed with the responsible jurisdiction.A Useful Request“For [address/parcel], please obtain [specific letter or existing record]. The intended use is [use]; the recipient needs answers to [questions]. Known permits or dates are [details]. Please confirm the responsible authority, fees, anticipated timing and any record limits.”Describe the Results and Documents AccuratelyAn existing occupancy certificate or permit copy is evidence from the issuing authority’s records. Obtaining the copy does not independently approve a new use or certify all work at the property. Keep any legal or technical assessment with the client’s advisers.Official sources and requirementsHillsborough County — Zoning verifications ↗Hillsborough County — Building permitting records ↗Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request.Information is drawn from the official sources linked here. Requirements, procedures and availability may change as jurisdictions update their rules. AADS confirms the requirements for your request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request Place an Order →","published":"REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026","sources":[{"label":"Hillsborough County — Zoning verifications ↗","url":"https://hcfl.gov/businesses/zoning/zoning-administrative-and-other-applications-and-forms/administrative-zoning-applications-and-forms-no-hearing/zoning-verifications-sign-offs-and-interpretations"},{"label":"Hillsborough County — Building permitting records ↗","url":"https://hcfl.gov/businesses/development-services-records/building-permitting-records-searches"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["general"]},{"title":"What should a criminal search include?","url":"./court-litigation-bankruptcy.html#criminal-research","topic":"courts","states":[],"question":"criminal misdemeanor felony traffic date of birth alias county state federal case dispositions","published":"Service guidance","sources":[{"label":"U.S. Courts: court locator","url":"https://www.uscourts.gov/court-locator"}],"reviewer":"Not assigned","sourceReview":"Not recorded","topics":["courts"]}]};
